0,"renderedHeight":1,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"DECORATIVE","altReason":"alt=\"\" (decorative)","bytes":821},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/images/spacer.gif","alt":" ","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":1,"renderedHeight":10,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"DECORATIVE","altReason":"alt=\"\" (decorative)","bytes":821},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/m844a2f1.gif","alt":"The figure shows the percentage of adults aged ≥18 years who were current smokers, by age group, in the United States from 1998 through 2008. Smoking prevalence was lowest among adults with a graduate degree (5.7%). Smoking prevalence did not vary significantly for adults aged 18-24 years (21.4%), 25-44 years (23.7%), and 45-64 years (22.6%); however, smoking prevalence was lower for adults aged ≥65 years (9.3%).","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":523,"renderedHeight":303,"naturalWidth":523,"naturalHeight":303,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 416 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":10841},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/m844a2f2.gif","alt":"The figure shows quit ratios among former smokers aged ≥25 years, by education in the United States from 1998 through 2008. In 2008, quit ratios were lower for adults aged ≥25 years with a General Education Development certificate (GED) (39.9%), adults with no high school diploma (45.7%), and adults with a high school diploma (48.8%), compared with quit ratios observed overall for adults aged ≥5 years (53.8%). During 1998-2008, the overall quit ratio was stable (or varied little) and ranged from 48.7% (1998) to 51.1% (2008). Persons with an undergraduate degree and persons with a graduate degree had quit ratios consistently higher than 60.0%. The only group with a significant upward linear trend in cessation was persons with a graduate degree; in 2008, the quit ratio was 80.7%, compared with 76.0% in 1998. Adults with a GED had the lowest quit ratio; during 1998-2008, their quit ratios ranged from 31.2% (2001) to 39.9% (2008).","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":517,"renderedHeight":397,"naturalWidth":517,"naturalHeight":397,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 940 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":17075},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/images/spacer.gif","alt":" ","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":10,"renderedHeight":1,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"DECORATIVE","altReason":"alt=\"\" (decorative)","bytes":821},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/headers_footers/images/spacer1.gif","alt":"","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":true,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":1,"renderedHeight":1,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"DECORATIVE","altReason":"alt=\"\" (decorative)","bytes":821},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/images/spacer1.gif","alt":"","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":true,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":1,"renderedHeight":1,"naturalWidth":10,"naturalHeight":10,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"DECORATIVE","altReason":"alt=\"\" (decorative)","bytes":821},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/images/s.gif","alt":"","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":true,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":20,"renderedHeight":1,"naturalWidth":1,"naturalHeight":1,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"DECORAT